.TH velgraph "" "Command"
.SH NAME
velgraph \- plot x/y data as a vellum drawing
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBvelgraph [\fB\-t\fR \fItitle\fR] [\fB\-x\fR \fIlabel\fR] [\fB\-y\fR \fIlabel\fR] \
[\fB\-smooth\fR] < \fIdata\fR > \fIgraph.d\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
velgraph -- plot x/y data as a vellum drawing
Usage:	velgraph [ -t title ] [ -x label ] [ -y label ] [ -smooth ] < data
Options:
	-t title	centred title (large font)
	-x label	x-axis label
	-y label	y-axis label (drawn vertically)
	-smooth	series render as splines, to pic and PostScript too
.HE
.B velgraph
reads
.I "x y"
pairs from standard input \- one pair per line, series separated by
blank lines, graph(1)'s convention \- and emits an ORDINARY
.B vellum
drawing: axes on the frame layer, ticks on the 1-2-5 ladder with
labels, and each series as a styled polyline (solid, dashed, dotted
and bold in turn).
A graph is not a special thing here \- it is objects: open the
result, annotate it, dimension it, stamp a frame on it, print it.
.PP
Input decimals parse to fixed point (no floating point anywhere);
ranges, tick steps and labels are integer arithmetic with the
decimal point re-inserted by the formatter.
The 1-2-5 ladder is exact in fixed point, which is precisely why it
is the 1-2-5 ladder and not ``nice numbers''.
With
.B \-smooth
each series carries the spline flag, so the curve is real all the
way to the
.B \-pic
and
.B \-ps
exports.
.PP
make(1) composition is the point:
.DS
.ta 8n
	report.ps: bench.log plot.d
		velgraph -t "Settling time" < bench.log > plot.d
		vellum -ps plot.d > report.ps
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B vellum ,
.B veldxf
.SH NOTES
Up to 280 points over up to 12 series; a series longer than a
polyline's 20 points is split into chained polylines sharing their
seam.
The sample gallery's /usr/vellum/eg/curve.d is a velgraph output,
annotated in the editor afterwards.
